  <main class="container main-content">

    <!-- Answer first -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="snapshot-heading">
      <h2 id="snapshot-heading">What a Free Divorce Consultation Covers</h2>
      <p>
        A free consultation here means the first meeting with the lawyer we connect you with costs you
        nothing, and you are free to hire that lawyer or not. DivorceU.AE is an information and
        referral service, not a law firm: we do not give legal advice ourselves, because UAE law
        reserves legal advice to licensed lawyers (more on that below). We use the details you send
        to connect you with a UAE-licensed family lawyer, and the appointment is arranged between you
        and the lawyer, in person, by phone or by video.
      </p>
      <p>
        By its nature the first consultation is introductory. The lawyer listens to your facts,
        identifies the law and court that apply and the routes open to you, and suggests the next
        step. Anything beyond that, such as written work or appearing for you, is paid work under a
        fee agreement made in writing before it starts.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>What it usually covers</h3>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li>Which law applies to you: the Muslim personal status law or the civil law for non-Muslims, and whether either of you can ask for your home country’s law.</li>
            <li>Which court is competent, and whether a UAE court can hear your case at all.</li>
            <li>Your realistic routes: an agreed divorce, khula, divorce for harm, or a civil divorce.</li>
            <li>Whether anything is urgent, such as temporary maintenance or a child’s travel.</li>
            <li>How the lawyer would charge if you need more help.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>What it usually does not cover</h3>
          <ul class="bullets">
            <li>Drafting a settlement agreement or a statement of claim.</li>
            <li>Reviewing a full file of documents and messages.</li>
            <li>A detailed written legal opinion.</li>
            <li>Appearing for you at Family Guidance or in court.</li>
            <li>A promised outcome or timeline, which no honest lawyer can give.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">Free</span>
          <span class="stat-label">first consultation arranged through DivorceU.AE</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">0</span>
          <span class="stat-label">obligation to hire the lawyer afterwards</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">Art. 6</span>
          <span class="stat-label">only licensed lawyers may give legal advice</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">In writing</span>
          <span class="stat-label">fee agreement before paid work starts (Art. 46(2))</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:24px;">
        A <strong>paid consultation</strong> is different: it is a session a firm charges for,
        usually because it goes further, for example by reviewing your documents or giving a written
        opinion. The price section below explains what sets that fee. This page covers the
        consultation only. For the full lawyer bill (agreed fee or hourly), see
        <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-fees-uae/">divorce lawyer fees in the UAE</a>; for court,
        translation and lawyer costs together, see the
        <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">total cost of divorce guide</a>. If you are not sure you need a
        lawyer at all, start with <a href="/en/do-you-need-a-lawyer-for-divorce-uae/">do you need a
        lawyer for divorce in the UAE</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>Contacting a firm directly? Confirm the fee before you book</h3>
        <p>
          Some firms offer a free call but charge for a longer meeting. Ask two questions when you
          book: is this meeting free or paid, and what will it cover. A consultation is also not an
          emergency service: if you or your children are in danger now, call the police on 999 first.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Who can advise -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="who-heading">
      <h2 id="who-heading">Who Can Legally Advise You on a Divorce in the UAE</h2>
      <p>
        Only a lawyer licensed in the UAE may practise law, use the title “lawyer”, give legal
        opinion and advice, draft contracts and appear for people before the courts, the public
        prosecution and the police. Courts and government bodies accept a lawyer’s representation
        only if the lawyer’s name is on the Roll of Practising Lawyers (Art. 6 of Federal Decree-Law
        No. 34 of 2022, the law regulating the legal profession and legal consultation). That is why
        the free consultation we arrange is with a licensed lawyer and not with us.
      </p>
      <p>
        The federal law also sets who can practise. A practising lawyer must be a UAE national
        (Art. 13(1)), aged at least 21 and meeting other conditions. A non-national lawyer can be licensed only
        after at least 15 years of practice, as a partner of a licensed foreign firm, and only before
        specialised circuits that exclude family and Muslim personal status cases (Art. 10).
        Emirates with their own judiciary, such as Dubai, may regulate the profession under local
        legislation (Art. 3(2)). In practice, the lawyer who appears for you in a family case is
        normally a UAE national, even if a legal consultant of another nationality met you first.
      </p>

      <h3>Legal consultants: what they can and cannot do</h3>
      <p>
        Many family law firms employ legal consultants alongside their lawyers, and a consultant may
        be the first person you meet. That is allowed, within limits. A legal researcher or
        consultant provides legal services only through the firm they work for, cannot plead or
        represent anyone before the courts or judicial committees, and their paperwork must be on the
        firm’s letterhead and signed by a lawyer on the firm’s roll (Art. 59). They may not sign
        statements of claim, sign fee agreements, or give consultations on their own account or for
        anyone other than their firm (Art. 61). Someone offering “legal consultations” outside any
        licensed firm, or calling themselves a lawyer when they are not, is a warning sign. In Dubai,
        the Legal Affairs Department of the Government of Dubai keeps separate rolls for advocates and
        for legal consultants.
      </p>

      <h3>Who else can appear for you in court</h3>
      <p>
        You do not need a lawyer to bring a case. Under the Civil Procedure Code (Federal Decree-Law
        No. 42 of 2022), parties may appear in person or through an attorney, who can be a lawyer or a
        relative or in-law up to the fourth degree (Art. 52). The legal profession law makes the same
        exception for a spouse, in-laws and blood relatives up to the fourth degree (Art. 8(1)), which
        is of little use in a divorce where your spouse is the other side. A relative can appear for
        you, but only a licensed lawyer can give you professional legal advice.
      </p>

      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Who</th><th>May</th><th>May not</th><th>Basis</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Lawyer on the Roll of Practising Lawyers</td><td>Give legal advice, draft agreements, appear and plead in court</td><td>Help the other side in the same dispute</td><td>Art. 6, Art. 45(1)(f)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Legal consultant or researcher</td><td>Provide legal services through their firm only</td><td>Plead in court, sign claims or fee agreements, advise on their own account</td><td>Art. 59, Art. 61</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Licensed non-national lawyer</td><td>Appear before designated specialised circuits</td><td>Act in family or Muslim personal status cases</td><td>Art. 10</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Relative or in-law up to the fourth degree</td><td>Appear for you before the courts</td><td>Practise law or use the title “lawyer”</td><td>Art. 6, Art. 8(1); CPC Art. 52</td></tr>
            <tr><td>You</td><td>Appear and file in person</td><td>No restriction</td><td>CPC Art. 52</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">
          Articles are from Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022 unless marked CPC (Federal Decree-Law
          No. 42 of 2022, Civil Procedure Code).
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>How to check that a lawyer is licensed</h3>
      <p>
        Ask for the lawyer’s name exactly as registered, their registration number and the courts they
        are admitted before, then check with the body that keeps that roll: the Ministry of Justice for
        the federal Roll of Practising Lawyers, and in Dubai the Legal Affairs Department; elsewhere,
        ask the lawyer which body holds their registration. If you meet
        someone at a firm, ask plainly: am I speaking to a lawyer or a legal consultant, and who will
        sign my fee agreement and attend my hearings? Our guide on
        <a href="/en/how-to-choose-a-divorce-lawyer-uae/">how to choose a divorce lawyer in the UAE</a>
        covers the rest of the checks, and the <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-dubai/">divorce lawyer
        Dubai guide</a> explains how cases run before the Dubai courts.
      </p>
      <p>
        Searching for divorce solicitors? The federal law on the legal profession does not use that
        title: it speaks of lawyers, legal consultants and legal researchers. The closest equivalent to
        a family solicitor in the UAE is a lawyer on the Roll of Practising Lawyers who handles family
        cases.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Confidentiality -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="secret-heading">
      <h2 id="secret-heading">Is What You Say in a Consultation Confidential?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, and it is a legal duty on the lawyer, not a courtesy. A lawyer may not disclose any secret
        entrusted to them or learnt through their work, whether you shared it orally, in writing, by
        email or through any other technology (Art. 45(1)(a) of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022).
        A lawyer must also refrain from giving statements about facts or information learnt through
        their work unless the person the information belongs to agrees (Art. 44).
      </p>
      <p>
        The exceptions are narrow: disclosure to prevent an offence against a person’s life or safety
        or serious damage to property, or where another law in force requires it. So speak openly. A
        lawyer can only advise you well on the full facts, including the ones that weaken your
        position, which are likely to come out in court anyway. Under our
        <a href="/en/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a>, what you send through our form is shared
        only with the lawyer or firm we connect you with.
      </p>

      <h3>Why you and your spouse cannot share a lawyer</h3>
      <p>
        A lawyer may not give any help, even in the form of advice, to a client’s opponent in the same
        dispute or a related one, and the ban continues after acting for that client ends
        (Art. 45(1)(f)). The same law also stops a lawyer from acting against someone who previously
        consulted them and showed them their documents and defence. What this means for you:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>If your spouse has already consulted a firm about the same dispute, that firm may have to decline you. That is its legal duty, not a judgment on you.</li>
        <li>Give your spouse’s full name when you book, so the firm can check for a conflict before you share any details.</li>
        <li>Even in a mutual-consent divorce, if one spouse’s lawyer drafts the settlement, the other spouse can have it checked by an independent lawyer before signing.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- What happens -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="happens-heading">
      <h2 id="happens-heading">What Actually Happens in a Consultation</h2>
      <p>
        A first consultation is a structured conversation, not a court hearing. Knowing its shape
        helps you use the time well, especially if you are paying for the session.
      </p>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>You explain your situation</h3>
          <p>
            You describe the marriage, whether both of you want a divorce, and any dispute over
            children, maintenance or property. A one-page timeline saves you minutes recalling dates.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>The lawyer identifies your track</h3>
          <p>
            They confirm which law applies: Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 for Muslims, in force
            since 15 April 2025, or Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 on civil personal status for
            non-Muslims, and whether either of you can ask for your home country’s law instead
            (Art. 1 of each law). They also confirm which court is competent and whether the case is
            agreed or contested. See <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">how divorce works in the UAE</a>
            for the full framework.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>You get a realistic read on your options</h3>
          <p>
            A good lawyer gives you a candid view on custody, maintenance and property rather than a
            promise. In a free introductory session this is a first view; a paid session or written
            opinion goes into more depth.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>You hear how the lawyer would charge</h3>
          <p>
            The lawyer explains whether they charge an agreed fee for the work or an hourly rate, and
            what the fee would cover. If you go ahead, the fee agreement must be in writing before
            the work starts (Art. 46(2) of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>You decide, with no obligation</h3>
          <p>
            You are not committed to hiring anyone after a consultation. Take the information, ask for
            a second opinion if you want one, and decide in your own time.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Paid fees -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="ranges-heading">
      <h2 id="ranges-heading">Paid Consultation Fees: What Sets the Price</h2>
      <p>
        If a firm charges for a consultation, the price is its own. We have not found an official
        tariff for consultation fees at federal level or in Dubai or Abu Dhabi, and the figures on
        law-firm websites are offers that change, so this page does not quote AED amounts. What the
        law does set is how a lawyer’s fee should be assessed. Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022 lists
        these factors (Art. 47):
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>The type and nature of the work, the effort it needs and the skills it calls for.</li>
        <li>The time the lawyer expects it to take.</li>
        <li>The importance of the case or of the interests in dispute.</li>
        <li>The lawyer’s experience, standing, seniority and level of registration, and the reputation of the firm.</li>
        <li>The firm’s costs of gathering information, and its other expenses.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Fees can be agreed in advance according to the type and nature of the work, or charged at the
        firm’s hourly rate. A fee agreement does not have to cover a whole case: it can be for giving a
        legal opinion or advice, drafting or reviewing a contract, one stage such as an appeal or
        enforcement, or a single procedural step (Art. 49(2)). So a paid consultation can be agreed on
        its own. Because the law requires the fee agreement to be in writing, by any means, before the
        agreed work starts (Art. 46(2)), ask for the price, the length of the session and what it
        includes in an email or message before you attend.
      </p>
      <p>
        Following those factors, expect a higher quote if the lawyer must review many documents before
        the meeting, if there are children, a business or assets abroad, or if you want a written
        opinion. Ask whether the fee is deducted from the case fee if you
        later hire the firm; that is for each firm to decide.
      </p>

      <h3>Before you sign a fee agreement for the whole case</h3>
      <p>
        Read the termination clause. If you remove the lawyer without a lawful reason after they have
        started the work, you owe the full agreed fee as if the work had been completed; if you remove
        them before they start, they are entitled to a fee for their preparatory effort of no more than
        25% of the agreed fee (Art. 57). That is one more reason to use the free consultation to compare
        calmly before you commit.
      </p>

      <h3>Is a paid consultation worth it?</h3>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>The free first consultation is usually enough when</h3>
          <p>
            You both agree on the divorce and on custody, maintenance and property, the case is a
            straightforward agreed filing, and you mainly need to know the route and what the lawyer
            would charge to handle it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>A paid session can earn its cost when</h3>
          <p>
            The divorce is contested, children are involved, there is property or a business to deal
            with, or there is a cross-border element such as assets or a spouse abroad. A documented
            review and a written opinion early on can shape every later decision.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        If money is tight, our <a href="/en/divorce-legal-aid-uae/">divorce legal aid in the UAE</a>
        guide covers free and low-cost options and who qualifies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Dubai and Abu Dhabi -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="cities-heading">
      <h2 id="cities-heading">How to Prepare for a Consultation in Dubai or Abu Dhabi</h2>
      <p>
        Prepare three things: copies of the key documents, a one-page timeline and a written list of
        questions. That is what turns a free introductory session into a useful one. Do not delay the
        consultation because a document is missing; copies are enough at this stage.
      </p>

      <h3>Documents to bring</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Marriage certificate. If it was issued abroad, ask the lawyer about the legalisation and legal Arabic translation the court file will need later.</li>
        <li>Both spouses’ passports and Emirates IDs, and your residence visa details.</li>
        <li>Children’s birth certificates.</li>
        <li>Proof of where you live, such as a tenancy contract.</li>
        <li>Proof of income and commitments: salary certificate, recent bank statements, loan instalments.</li>
        <li>Papers for property, vehicles, accounts and companies, in the UAE and abroad.</li>
        <li>Any prenuptial, postnuptial or separation agreement.</li>
        <li>Any paper you have received from a court or from Family Guidance, with the date you received it.</li>
        <li>Messages, medical reports or police reports, if any; ask the lawyer how they can lawfully be used.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If a divorce has already been pronounced, write down when and how. The husband must document a
        divorce before the competent court within 15 days of it occurring, which does not affect the
        wife’s right to file a case to prove it; if he does not, without an excuse the court accepts,
        she is entitled to compensation equal to maintenance from the date of the divorce to the date
        it is documented (Art. 58 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). Dates like these change the
        advice, so do not leave them to memory. The full filing checklist is in
        <a href="/en/documents-for-divorce-uae/">documents required for divorce in the UAE</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Dubai: the court side</h3>
          <p>
            Family cases are heard by the Personal Status Court of Dubai Courts, in Deira, Al Garhoud
            area, Street 5, next to the RTA main building. Dubai Courts list its hours as Monday to
            Thursday 8:00 am to 3:00 pm and Friday 8:00 am to 11:30 am, and the call centre is
            +971 4 334 7777; courts close at weekends and on public holidays, so check before you go.
            Personal status claims are filed through the Dubai Courts website or an Al Adheed service
            centre, with documents uploaded as PDFs with a legal Arabic translation, and foreign
            documents legalised by the issuing country’s foreign ministry, the UAE embassy there and
            then the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs. The court fee under Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015 is
            AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000; confirm it when you register.
          </p>
          <a href="/en/family-court-dubai/" class="card-link">Family Court Dubai guide →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Abu Dhabi: the court side</h3>
          <p>
            Family courts in Abu Dhabi belong to the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department. Non-Muslims can
            use the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, which runs English-language proceedings and charges
            AED 5,000 for a civil divorce application. Muslim family cases are heard by the Judicial
            Department’s courts, normally under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024. Check the address,
            hours and current fees on the Judicial Department’s website or in our Abu Dhabi family
            court guide before you go, and tell the lawyer at the consultation which court you expect
            to use.
          </p>
          <a href="/en/family-court-abu-dhabi/" class="card-link">Family Court Abu Dhabi guide →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:24px;">
        For lawyers in each city, see the <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-dubai/">divorce lawyer Dubai</a>
        and <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-abu-dhabi/">divorce lawyer Abu Dhabi</a> guides, and for the
        filing steps, <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">how to file in Dubai</a> and
        <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file in Abu Dhabi</a>. Filing
        elsewhere? Consult a lawyer admitted before the courts that will hear your case. Cases in
        Ajman, Fujairah and Umm Al Quwain go to the federal courts; Ras Al Khaimah has its own local
        courts, and Sharjah has had its own judiciary since 2025. See our guides for
        <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-sharjah/">Sharjah</a>,
        <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-ras-al-khaimah/">Ras Al Khaimah</a>,
        <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-ajman/">Ajman</a>,
        <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-fujairah/">Fujairah</a> and
        <a href="/en/divorce-lawyer-umm-al-quwain/">Umm Al Quwain</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>Language and the consultation</h3>
        <p>
          If you need the meeting in English or Arabic, say so when you request it. A lawyer you can
          follow without effort is worth more than a slightly lower fee.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Questions to ask -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="ask-heading">
      <h2 id="ask-heading">The Exact Questions to Ask</h2>
      <p>
        Use the consultation to interview the lawyer, not just to explain your case. Clear answers to
        these questions are a good sign. Vague ones are a warning.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Is this consultation free or paid? If paid, how long is it, what does it include, and is the fee deducted if I hire you?</li>
        <li>Are you a lawyer on the Roll of Practising Lawyers or a legal consultant, which courts are you admitted before, and what is your registration number?</li>
        <li>Who will sign the fee agreement, attend my hearings and handle my file day to day?</li>
        <li>Which law and which court apply to my case, and can either of us ask for our home country’s law?</li>
        <li>Do I need anything urgent now, such as temporary maintenance or an order about a child’s travel?</li>
        <li>How will you charge for the case: an agreed fee for the work or an hourly rate? What is excluded, such as appeals, enforcement, translation or expert fees?</li>
        <li>What will the written fee agreement say, and when will I receive it?</li>
        <li>What happens to the fee if the case settles early or I end the engagement?</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="cta-inline">
        <p>Want a free first consultation with a licensed lawyer?</p>
